"Basic Law for the Federal Republic of Germany (https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/englisch_gg/englisch_gg.html)\nciteturn21view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/englisch_gg/englisch_gg.html\",\"pattern\":\"Article 67\"}); Total lines: 2115\nL640: Article 55\nL641: [Incompatibilities]\nL642: \nL643: (1) The Federal President may not be a member of the government or of a legislative body of the Federation or of a Land.\nL644: \nL645: (2) The Federal President may not hold any other salaried office or engage in any trade or profession or belong to the management or supervisory board of any enterprise conducted for profit.\nL646: \nL651: On assuming his office, the Federal President shall take the following oath before the assembled Members of the Bundestag and the Bundesrat:\nL652: \nL653: “I swear that I will dedicate my efforts to the well-being of the German people, promote their welfare, protect them from harm, uphold and defend the Basic Law and the laws of the Federation, perform my duties conscientiously and do justice to all. So help me God.”\nL654: \nL655: The oath may also be taken without religious affirmation.\nL656: \nL657: 【73†table of contents】\nL658: Article 57\nL659: [Substitution]\nL660: \nL661: If the Federal President is unable to perform his duties, or if his office falls prematurely vacant, the President of the Bundesrat shall exercise his powers.\nL662: \nL663: 【74†table of contents】\nL664: \nL665: Article 58\nL666: [Countersignature]\nL667: Orders and directions of the Federal President shall require for their validity the countersignature of the Federal Chancellor or of the competent Federal Minister. This provision shall not apply to the appointment or dismissal of the Federal Chancellor, the dissolution of the Bundestag under Article 63, or a request made under paragraph (3) of Article 69.\nL668: \nL669: 【75†table of contents】\nL670: \nL671: Article 59\nL672: [International representation of the Federation]\nL673: (1) The Federal President shall represent the Federation in international law. He shall conclude treaties with foreign states on behalf of the Federation. He shall accredit and receive envoys.\nL674: (2) Treaties that regulate the political relations of the Federation or relate to subjects of federal legislation shall require the consent or participation, in the form of a federal law, of the bodies responsible in such a case for the enactment of federal law. In the case of executive agreements the provisions concerning the federal administration shall apply, mutatis mutandis.\nL675: \nL676: 【76†table of contents】\nL677: \nL678: Article 59a\nL679: (repealed)\nL680: \nL681: 【77†table of contents】\nL682: Article 60\nL683: [Appointment of civil servants – Pardon – Immunity]\nL684: \nL685: (1) The Federal President shall appoint and dismiss federal judges, federal civil servants and commissioned and non-commissioned officers of the Armed Forces, except as may otherwise be provided by a law.\nL686: \nL687: (2) He shall exercise the power to pardon offenders on behalf of the Federation in individual cases.\nL688: \nL689: (3) He may delegate these powers to other authorities.\nL690: (4) Paragraphs (2) to (4) of Article 46 shall apply to the Federal President, mutatis mutandis.\nL691: \nL692: 【78†table of contents】\nL693: \nL694: Article 61\nL695: [Impeachment before the Federal Constitutional Court]\nL696: (1) The Bundestag or the Bundesrat may impeach the Federal President before the Federal Constitutional Court for wilful violation of this Basic Law or of any other federal law. The motion of impeachment must be supported by at least one quarter of the Members of the Bundestag or one quarter of the votes of the Bundesrat. The decision to impeach shall require a majority of two thirds of the Members of the Bundestag or of two thirds of the votes of the Bundesrat.\nL697: The case for impeachment shall be presented before the Federal Constitutional Court by a person commissioned by the impeaching body.\nL698: (2) If the Federal Constitutional Court finds the Federal President guilty of a wilful violation of this Basic Law or of any other federal law, it may declare that he has forfeited his office. After the Federal President has been impeached, the Court may issue an interim order preventing him from exercising his functions.\nL699: \nL700: 【79†table of contents】\nL701: \nL702: VI. The Federal Government\nL703: \nL704: 【80†table of contents】\nL705: \nL706: Article 62\nL707: [Composition]\nL708: The Federal Government shall consist of the Federal Chancellor and the Federal Ministers.\nL709: \nL710: 【81†table of contents】\nL711: \nL712: Article 63\nL713: [Election of the Federal Chancellor]\nL714: \nL715: (1) The Federal Chancellor shall be elected by the Bundestag without debate on the proposal of the Federal President.\nL716: \nL717: (2) The person who receives the votes of a majority of the Members of the Bundestag shall be elected. The person elected shall be appointed by the Federal President.\nL718: (3) If the person proposed by the Federal President is not elected, the Bundestag may elect a Federal Chancellor within fourteen days after the ballot by the votes of more than one half of its Members.\nL719: (4) If no Federal Chancellor is elected within this period, a new election shall take place without delay, in which the person who receives the largest number of votes shall be elected. If the person elected receives the votes of a majority of the Members of the Bundestag, the Federal President must appoint him within seven days after the election. If the person elected does not receive such a majority, then within seven days the Federal President shall either appoint him or dissolve the Bundestag.\nL720: 【82†table of contents】\nL721: \nL722: Article 64\nL723: [Appointment and dismissal of Federal Ministers – Oath of office]\nL724: \nL725: (1) Federal Ministers shall be appointed and dismissed by the Federal President upon the proposal of the Federal Chancellor.\nL726: \nL727: (2) On taking office the Federal Chancellor and the Federal Ministers shall take the oath provided for in Article 56 before the Bundestag.\nL728: \nL729: 【83†table of contents】\nL730: \nL731: Article 65\nL732: [Power to determine policy guidelines – Department and collegiate responsibility]\nL733: The Federal Chancellor shall determine and be responsible for the general guidelines of policy. Within these limits each Federal Minister shall conduct the affairs of his department independently and on his own responsibility. The Federal Government shall resolve differences of opinion between Federal Ministers. The Federal Chancellor shall conduct the proceedings of the Federal Government in accordance with rules of procedure adopted by the Government and approved by the Federal President.\nL734: 【84†table of contents】\nL735: \nL736: Article 65a\nL737: [Command of the Armed Forces]\nL738: \nL739: (1) Command of the Armed Forces shall be vested in the Federal Minister of Defence.\nL740: \nL741: (2) (repealed)\nL742: \nL743: 【85†table of contents】\nL744: \nL745: Article 66\nL746: [Incompatibilities]\nL747: \nL748: Neither the Federal Chancellor nor a Federal Minister may hold any other salaried office or engage in any trade or profession or belong to the management or, without the consent of the Bundestag, to the supervisory board of an enterprise conducted for profit.\nL749: \nL750: 【86†table of contents】\nL751: Article 67\nL752: [Vote of no confidence]\nL753: \nL754: (1) The Bundestag may express its lack of confidence in the Federal Chancellor only by electing a successor by the vote of a majority of its Members and requesting the Federal President to dismiss the Federal Chancellor. The Federal President must comply with the request and appoint the person elected.\nL755: \nL756: (2) Forty-eight hours shall elapse between the motion and the election.\nL757: \nL758: 【87†table of contents】\nL759: \nL760: Article 68\nL761: [Vote of confidence]\nL762: (1) If a motion of the Federal Chancellor for a vote of confidence is not supported by the majority of the Members of the Bundestag, the Federal President, upon the proposal of the Federal Chancellor, may dissolve the Bundestag within twenty-one days. The right of dissolution shall lapse as soon as the Bundestag elects another Federal Chancellor by the vote of a majority of its Members.\nL763: \nL764: (2) Forty-eight hours shall elapse between the motion and the vote.\nL765: \nL766: 【88†table of contents】\nL767: Article 69\nL768: [Deputy Federal Chancellor – Term of office]\nL769: \nL770: (1) The Federal Chancellor shall appoint a Federal Minister as his deputy.\nL771: \nL772: (2) The tenure of office of the Federal Chancellor or of a Federal Minister shall end in any event when a new Bundestag convenes; the tenure of office of a Federal Minister shall also end on any other occasion on which the Federal Chancellor ceases to hold office.\nL773: (3) At the request of the Federal President the Federal Chancellor, or at the request of the Federal Chancellor or of the Federal President a Federal Minister, shall be obliged to continue to manage the affairs of his office until a successor is appointed.\nL774: \nL775: 【89†table of contents】\nL776: \nL777: VII. Federal Legislation and Legislative Procedures\nL778: \nL779: 【90†table of contents】\nL780: \nL781: Article 70\nL782: [Division of powers between the Federation and the Länder]\nL783: (1) The Länder shall have the right to legislate insofar as this Basic Law does not confer legislative power on the Federation.\nL784: \nL785: (2) The division of authority between the Federation and the Länder shall be governed by the provisions of this Basic Law concerning exclusive and concurrent legislative powers.\nL786: \nL787: 【91†table of contents】\nL788: \nL789: Article 71\nL790: [Exclusive legislative power of the Federation]\nL791: On matters within the exclusive legislative power of the Federation, the Länder shall have power to legislate only when and to the extent that they are expressly authorised to do so by a federal law.\nL792: \nL793: 【92†table of contents】\nL794: \nL795: Article 72\nL796: [Concurrent legislative powers]\nL797: \nL798: (1) On matters within the concurrent legislative power, the Länder shall have power to legislate so long as and to the extent that the Federation has not exercised its legislative power by enacting a law.\nL799: (2) The Federation shall have the right to legislate on matters falling within items 4, 7, 11, 13, 15, 19a, 20, 22, 25 and 26 of paragraph (1) of Article 74, if and to the extent that the establishment of equivalent living conditions throughout the federal territory or the maintenance of legal or economic unity renders federal regulation necessary in the national interest.\nL800: (3) If the Federation has made use of its power to legislate, the Länder may enact laws at variance with this legislation with respect to:\nL801: \nL802: 1.  hunting (except for the law on hunting licences);\nL803: \nL804: 2.  protection of nature and landscape management (except for the general principles governing the protection of nature, the law on protection of plant and animal species or the law on protection of marine life);\nL805: \nL806: 3.  land distribution;\nL807: \nL808: 4.  regional planning;\nL809: 5.  management of water resources (except for regulations related to materials or facilities);\nL810: \nL811: 6.  admission to institutions of higher education and requirements for graduation in such institutions;\nL812: \nL813: 7.  taxes on real property.\nL814: Federal laws on these matters shall enter into force no earlier than six months following their promulgation unless otherwise provided with the consent of the Bundesrat. As for the relationship between federal law and law of the Länder, the latest law enacted shall take precedence with respect to matters within the scope of the first sentence.\nL815: (4) A federal law may provide that federal legislation which is no longer necessary within the meaning of paragraph (2) of this Article may be superseded by Land law.\nL816: \nL817: 【93†table of contents】\nL818: \nL819: Article 73\nL820: [Matters under exclusive legislative power of the Federation]\nL821: \nL822: (1) The Federation shall have exclusive legislative power with respect to:\nL823: \nL824: 1.  foreign affairs and defence, including protection of the civilian population;\nL825: \nL826: 2.  citizenship in the Federation;\nL827: 3.  freedom of movement, passports, residency registration and identity cards, immigration, emigration and extradition;\nL828: \nL829: 4.  currency, money and coinage, weights and measures, and the determination of standards of time;\nL830: \nL831: 5.  the unity of the customs and trading area, treaties regarding commerce and navigation, the free movement of goods, and the exchange of goods and payments with foreign countries, including customs and border protection;\nL832: 5a.  safeguarding German cultural assets against removal from the country;\nL833: \nL834: 6.  air transport;\nL835: \nL836: 6a.  the operation of railways wholly or predominantly owned by the Federation (federal railways), the construction, maintenance and operation of railway lines belonging to federal railways and the levying of charges for the use of these lines;\nL837: \nL838: 7.  postal and telecommunications services;\nL839: \nL840: 8.  the legal relations of persons employed by the Federation and by federal corporations under public law;\nL841: 9.  industrial property rights, copyrights and publishing;\nL842: \nL843: 9a.  protection by the Federal Criminal Police Office against the dangers of international terrorism when a threat transcends the boundary of one Land, when responsibility is not clearly assignable to the police authorities of any particular Land or when the highest authority of an individual Land requests the assumption of federal responsibility;\nL844: \nL845: 10.  cooperation between the Federation and the Länder concerning\nL846: \nL847: (a)  criminal police work,\nL848: (b)  protection of the free democratic basic order, existence and security of the Federation or of a Land (protection of the constitution), and\nL849: \nL850: (c)  protection against activities within the federal territory which, by the use of force or preparations for the use of force, endanger the external interests of the Federal Republic of Germany,\nL851: \nL852: as well as the establishment of a Federal Criminal Police Office and international action to combat crime;\nL853: \nL854: 11.  statistics for federal purposes;\nL855: 12.  the law on weapons and explosives;\nL856: \nL857: 13.  benefits for persons disabled by war and for dependents of deceased war victims as well as assistance to former prisoners of war;\nL858: \nL859: 14.  the production and utilisation of nuclear energy for peaceful purposes, the construction and operation of facilities serving such purposes, protection against hazards arising from the release of nuclear energy or from ionising radiation, and the disposal of radioactive substances.\nL860: (2) Laws enacted pursuant to item 9a of paragraph (1) require the consent of the Bundesrat.\nL861: \nL862: 【94†table of contents】\nL863: \nL864: Article 74\nL865: [Matters under concurrent legislative powers]\nL866: \nL867: (1) Concurrent legislative power shall extend to the following matters:\nL868: \nL869: 1.  civil law, criminal law, court organisation and procedure (except for the law governing pre-trial detention), the legal profession, notaries and the provision of legal advice;\nL870: \nL871: 2.  registration of births, deaths and marriages;\nL872: \nL873: 3.  the law of association;\nL874: 4.  the law relating to residence and establishment of foreign nationals;\nL875: \nL876: 4a.  (repealed)\nL877: \nL878: 5.  (repealed)\nL879: \nL880: 6.  matters concerning refugees and expellees;\nL881: \nL882: 7.  public welfare (except for the law on social care homes);\nL883: \nL884: 8.  (repealed)\nL885: \nL886: 9.  war damage and reparations;\nL887: \nL888: 10.  war graves and graves of other victims of war or despotism;\nL889: 11.  the law relating to economic matters (mining, industry, energy, crafts, trades, commerce, banking, stock exchanges and private insurance), except for the law on shop closing hours, restaurants, amusement arcades, display of persons, trade fairs, exhibitions and markets;\nL890: \nL891: 12.  labour law, including the organisation of enterprises, occupational health and safety and employment agencies, as well as social security, including unemployment insurance;\nL892: 13.  the regulation of educational and training grants and the promotion of research;\nL893: \nL894: 14.  the law regarding expropriation, to the extent relevant to matters enumerated in Articles 73 and 74;\nL895: \nL896: 15.  the transfer of land, natural resources and means of production to public ownership or other forms of public enterprise;\nL897: \nL898: 16.  prevention of the abuse of economic power;\nL899: 17.  the promotion of agricultural production and forestry (except for the law on land consolidation), ensuring the adequacy of food supply, the importation and exportation of agricultural and forestry products, deep-sea and coastal fishing and coastal preservation;\nL900: \nL901: 18.  urban real estate transactions, land law (except for laws regarding development fees), and the law on rental subsidies, subsidies for old debts, homebuilding loan premiums, miners’ homebuilding and pit villages;\nL902: 19.  measures to combat human and animal diseases which pose a danger to the public or are communicable, admission to the medical profession and to ancillary professions or occupations, as well as the law on pharmacies, medicines, medical products, drugs, narcotics and poisons;\nL903: \nL904: 19a.  the economic viability of hospitals and the regulation of hospital charges;\nL905: 20.  the law on food products including animals used in their production, the law on alcohol and tobacco, essential commodities and feedstuffs as well as protective measures in connection with the marketing of agricultural and forest seeds and seedlings, the protection of plants against diseases and pests, as well as the protection of animals;\nL906: 21.  maritime and coastal shipping, as well as navigational aids, inland navigation, meteorological services, sea routes and inland waterways used for general traffic;\nL907: \nL908: 22.  road traffic, motor transport, construction and maintenance of long-distance highways, as well as the collection of tolls for the use of public highways by vehicles and the allocation of the revenue;\nL909: \nL910: 23.  non-federal railways, except mountain railways;\nL911: 24.  waste disposal, air pollution control, and noise abatement (except for the protection from noise associated with human activity);\nL912: \nL913: 25.  state liability;\nL914: \nL915: 26.  medically assisted generation of human life, analysis and modification of genetic information as well as the regulation of organ, tissue and cell transplantation;\nL916: 27.  the statutory rights and duties of civil servants of the Länder, the municipalities and other corporations established under public law as well as of the judges in the Länder, except for their career regulations, remuneration and pensions;\nL917: \nL918: 28.  hunting;\nL919: \nL920: 29.  protection of nature and landscape management;\nL921: \nL922: 30.  land distribution;\nL923: \nL924: 31.  regional planning;\nL925: \nL926: 32.  management of water resources;\nL927: \nL928: 33.  admission to institutions of higher education and requirements for graduation in such institutions.\nL929: (2) Laws enacted pursuant to items 25 and 27 of paragraph (1) shall require the consent of the Bundesrat.\nL930: \nL931: 【95†table of contents】\nL932: \nL933: Article 74a\nL934: (repealed)\nL935: \nL936: 【96†table of contents】\nL937: \nL938: Article 75\nL939: (repealed)\nL940: \nL941: 【97†table of contents】\nL942: \nL943: Article 76\nL944: [Bills]\nL945: \nL946: (1) Bills may be introduced in the Bundestag by the Federal Government, by the Bundesrat or from the floor of the Bundestag.\nL947: (2) Federal Government bills shall first be submitted to the Bundesrat. The Bundesrat shall be entitled to comment on such bills within six weeks. If for important reasons, especially with respect to the scope of the bill, the Bundesrat demands an extension, the period shall be increased to nine weeks.\nL948: If in exceptional circumstances the Federal Government, on submitting a bill to the Bundesrat, declares it to be particularly urgent, it may submit the bill to the Bundestag after three weeks or, if the Bundesrat has demanded an extension pursuant to the third sentence of this paragraph, after six weeks, even if it has not yet received the Bundesrat’s comments; upon receiving such comments, it shall transmit them to the Bundestag without delay.\nL949: In the case of bills to amend this Basic Law or to transfer sovereign powers pursuant to Article 23 or 24, the comment period shall be nine weeks; the fourth sentence of this paragraph shall not apply.\nL950: (3) Bundesrat bills shall be submitted to the Bundestag by the Federal Government within six weeks. In submitting them the Federal Government shall state its own views. If for important reasons, especially with respect to the scope of the bill, the Federal Government demands an extension, the period shall be increased to nine weeks.\nL951: If in exceptional circumstances the Bundesrat declares a bill to be particularly urgent, the period shall be three weeks or, if the Federal Government has demanded an extension pursuant to the third sentence of this paragraph, six weeks. In the case of bills to amend this Basic Law or to transfer sovereign powers pursuant to Article 23 or 24, the comment period shall be nine weeks; the fourth sentence of this paragraph shall not apply.\nL952: The Bundestag shall consider and vote on bills within a reasonable time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nShadow Cabinet - UK Parliament (https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/shadow-cabinet/)\nciteturn21view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/shadow-cabinet/\",\"pattern\":\"The shadow cabinet\"}); Total lines: 230\nL0: 【0††www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Skip to main content】 【2†Menu】\nL3: \nL4: Menu\nL5: \nL6: Select an area to explore\nL7: \nL8:   * 【2†Business】\nL9:   * 【2†MPs, Lords & offices】\nL10:   * 【2†About】\nL11:   * 【2†Get involved】\nL12:   * 【2†Visit】\nL13:   * 【2†Learning】\nL14:   * 【3†Shop†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL15:   * 【2†More】\nL16: \nL17:   * Or type a keyword to search\nL18: \nL19: [Input: Search UK Parliament]\nL20: \nL21: [Button: Search]\nL22: \nL23: 【2†Close】\nL24: \nL25: [Input]\nL26: \nL27: 【2†Close】\nL28: ## Parliamentary business\nL29: \nL30: Find out what’s on today at the House of Commons and House of Lords. Track current bills, keep up with committees, watch live footage and follow topical issues.\nL31: \nL32: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL33: ### In this section\nL34: \nL35:   * 【4†Parliamentary business home】\nL36:   * 【5†House of Commons】\nL37:   * 【6†House of Lords】\nL38:   * 【7†What's on†calendar.parliament.uk】\nL39:   * 【8†Bills & legislation】\nL40:   * 【9†Committees†committees.parliament.uk】\nL41:   * 【10†Publications & records】\nL42:   * 【11†Parliament TV†www.parliamentlive.tv】\nL43:   * 【12†News from UK Parliament】\nL44: ### Legislation and debate\nL45: \nL46: 【13†Hansard Read transcripts of debates in both Houses.†hansard.parliament.uk】 【14†Research briefings Produced by Commons Library, Lords Library, and Parliamentary Office Science and Technology.†researchbriefings.parliament.uk】\nL47: ## MPs, Lords & offices\nL48: \nL49: MPs and Members of the Lords sit in the two Chambers of Parliament scrutinising the Government and debating legislation. Find Members of Parliament (MPs) by postcode and constituency, and Members of the House of Lords by name and party.\nL50: \nL51: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL52: ### In this section\nL53: \nL54:   * 【15†MPs, Lords & offices home】\nL55:   * 【16†MPs†members.parliament.uk】\nL56:   * 【17†Members of the House of Lords†members.parliament.uk】\nL57:   * 【18†Government & opposition†members.parliament.uk】\nL58:   * 【19†Parliamentary offices】\nL59:   * 【20†Members' allowances】\nL60:   * 【21†Standards and financial interests】\nL61: ### Members of Parliament\nL62: \nL63: 【22†Find your MP Search for Members by name, postcode, or constituency†members.parliament.uk】 【17†Search for Members of the House of Lords Learn about their experience, knowledge and interests.†members.parliament.uk】\nL64: \nL65: ## About Parliament\nL66: \nL67: Parliament examines what the Government is doing, makes new laws, holds the power to set taxes and debates the issues of the day.\nL68: \nL69: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL70: ### In this section\nL71: \nL72:   * 【23†About Parliament home】\nL73:   * 【24†How Parliament works】\nL74:   * 【25†About MPs, Lords & officers】\nL75:   * 【26†Independent Complaints and Grievance Scheme (ICGS)】\nL76:   * 【27†Living Heritage】\nL77:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL78:   * 【29†Frequently Asked Questions】\nL79:   * 【30†Sustainability and environmental performance in Parliament】\nL80:   * 【31†UK Parliament's Modern Slavery Programme】\nL81: ## Get involved\nL82: \nL83: Read about how to contact an MP or Lord, petition Parliament and find out details of events in your area.\nL84: \nL85: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL86: \nL87: ### In this section\nL88: \nL89:   * 【32†Get involved home】\nL90:   * 【33†Contact an MP or a Member of the Lords】\nL91:   * 【34†Join a learning programme】\nL92:   * 【35†Research impact at the UK Parliament】\nL93:   * 【36†Have your say: Laws and debates】\nL94:   * 【37†Y Gornel Gymraeg】\nL95: ### Get involved with Parliament\nL96: \nL97: 【33†Contact an MP or Lord Contact your MP or a Member of the House of Lords about an issue that matters to you.】 【38†Keep up to date Sign up for the Your Parliament newsletter to find out how you can get involved.†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL98: \nL99: ### It starts with action\nL100: \nL101:   * 【39†Sign or create a petition】\nL102:   * 【40†Sign up to UK Parliament Week†www.ukparliamentweek.org】\nL103: ## Visit Parliament\nL104: \nL105: Find out how to take a tour in person, join an online event, book tickets and plan a visit to UK Parliament.\nL106: \nL107: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL108: \nL109: ### In this section\nL110: \nL111:   * 【41†Visit Parliament home】\nL112:   * 【42†Tours】\nL113:   * 【43†Talks and events】\nL114:   * 【44†Watch debates and committees】\nL115:   * 【45†Plan your visit】\nL116:   * 【46†360° virtual tour】\nL117:   * 【47†Venue hire】\nL118:   * 【3†Parliament Shop†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL119: ### Keep up to date\nL120: \nL121: 【48†Visit newsletter Sign up to our newsletter to receive the latest news about visiting UK Parliament.†parliament.us14.list-manage.com】\nL122: \nL123: ## Learning\nL124: \nL125: Your UK Parliament offers free, flexible support for teachers, community groups, and home educators to spark engagement and active citizenship.\nL126: \nL127: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL128: ### In this section\nL129: \nL130:   * 【49†Learning home†learning.parliament.uk】\nL131:   * 【50†About UK Parliament education†learning.parliament.uk】\nL132:   * 【51†Teacher resources and lesson plans†learning.parliament.uk】\nL133:   * 【52†CPD Training†learning.parliament.uk】\nL134:   * 【53†Outreach†learning.parliament.uk】\nL135:   * 【54†Visit†learning.parliament.uk】\nL136: ### Supporting teachers\nL137: \nL138: 【54†Inspire your students Book a school visit, classroom workshop or teacher-training session†learning.parliament.uk】 【55†Free teacher training resources Access videos, worksheets, lesson plans and games.†learning.parliament.uk】\nL139: \nL140: ### Keep up to date\nL141: \nL142:   * 【56†Sign up to our education newsletter†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL143: \nL144: ## Shop\nL145: \nL146: Shop\nL147: \nL148: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL149: \nL150: ### In this section\nL151: \nL152:   * 【3†Shop home†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL153: \nL154: 【57†UK Parliament】\nL155: # Shadow Cabinet\nL156: \nL157:   1. 【58†All】\nL158:   2. 【59†A】\nL159:   3. 【60†B】\nL160:   4. 【61†C】\nL161:   5. 【62†D】\nL162:   6. 【63†E】\nL163:   7. 【64†F】\nL164:   8. 【65†G】\nL165:   9. 【66†H】\nL166:   10. 【67†I】\nL167:   11. 【68†J】\nL168:   12. 【69†K】\nL169:   13. 【70†L】\nL170:   14. 【71†M】\nL171:   15. 【72†N】\nL172:   16. 【73†O】\nL173:   17. 【74†P】\nL174:   18. 【75†Q】\nL175:   19. 【76†R】\nL176:   20. 【77†S】\nL177:   21. 【78†T】\nL178:   22. 【79†U】\nL179:   23. 【80†V】\nL180:   24. 【81†W】\nL181:   25. 【82†X】\nL182:   26. 【83†Y】\nL183:   27. 【84†Z】\nL184: The Shadow Cabinet is the team of senior spokespeople chosen by the Leader of the Opposition to mirror the Cabinet in Government. Each member of the shadow cabinet is appointed to lead on a specific policy area for their party and to question and challenge their counterpart in the Cabinet. In this way the Official Opposition seeks to present itself as an alternative government-in-waiting.\nL185: \nL186:   * 【85†List of HM Official Opposition†members.parliament.uk】\nL187:   * 【86†Related glossary term: Cabinet】\nL188: ## Find out more\nL189: \nL190: ### Who works in Parliament?\nL191: \nL192:   * 【15†MPs, Lords and Offices】\nL193: \nL194: ###### Popular\nL195: \nL196:   * 【87†What's on in Parliament†whatson.parliament.uk】\nL197:   * 【88†Petitions†petition.parliament.uk】\nL198:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL199:   * 【89†Commons Media Relations】\nL200:   * 【90†Lords Press Office】\nL201:   * 【91†Contact us】\nL202: \nL203: ###